        "id": "cex-qft-formal-factorial-series",
        "kind": "counterexample",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Every formal coefficient exists while convergence radius vanishes",
        "reference": "research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/quantum-field-theory/proposed-inventory.json#cex-qft-formal-factorial-series; proof module research/extended-frameworks-2026-10-03/quantum-field-theory/complete-local-arguments.md#C5",
        "design_state": "reserved-QFT-MC03 B-leaf counterexample; complete local argument",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [],
        "dependency_roles": {},
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Define $\\mathbb C\\llbracket g\\rrbracket$ to be the set of coefficient sequences $(a_n)_{n\\ge0}$, written $\\sum_{n\\ge0}a_ng^n$, with coefficientwise equality and addition and Cauchy multiplication: the coefficient of $g^n$ in a product is $\\sum_{k=0}^na_kb_{n-k}$, a finite sum. Formal membership makes no numerical convergence assertion. For numerical evaluation use $g^0:=1$ for every $g$, including $g=0$; numerical convergence means convergence of the partial sums in $\\mathbb C$. The numerical radius of a coefficient sequence is $R:=\\sup\\{r\\ge0: \\sum_{n\\ge0}a_ng^n\\text{ converges for every }g\\in\\mathbb C\\text{ with }|g|<r\\}$. The claim to refute is that every element of $\\mathbb C\\llbracket g\\rrbracket$ has $R>0$.",
        "strategy": "Use the coefficient sequence n! to define a formal series, then show its numerical terms fail the necessary term test for every nonzero complex coupling.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-generated",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "generation": {
          "role": "counterexample"
        },
        "sources": {
